syntax: give && precedence over || and ! over both in [[ ]]

bash, mksh, ksh93 and zsh evaluate a [[ ]] expression with ! binding
tighter than &&, and && tighter than ||:

	[[ -n "" && -n "" || -n x ]]   # true: (F && F) || T
	[[ ! -n "" && -n "" ]]         # false: (!F) && F

The parser read every && and || with the same precedence, grouping
from the right, and let ! take everything after it, so the first test
became -n "" && (-n "" || -n x) and the second !(-n "" && -n ""),
which evaluate to the opposite of what the shells do. Printing was not
affected, but any tool that interprets the tree was.

Expressions are now parsed as || lists of && lists of single tests, and
! applies to one test, such as the comparison in ! a == b. Lists of one
operator still group from the right, so trees without a mix of
operators are unchanged.

Reproducer, before this change: `[[ a && b || c ]]` parsed as a
BinaryTest with Op AndTest whose Y was the OrTest b || c.

